// agent/models.json — pi custom model registry
//
// Pi's built-in `github-copilot` provider catalog is hardcoded per pi release
// (docs/providers.md: "For each provider, pi knows all available models. The
// list is updated with every pi release."). This file lets us add Copilot
// models ahead of the next pi release using pi's documented merge-by-id
// semantics:
//
//   - Built-in models are kept.
//   - Custom models are upserted by `id` within the provider.
//   - If a custom model `id` matches a built-in model `id`, the custom model
//     replaces that built-in model.
//   - If a custom model `id` is new, it is added alongside built-in models.
//
// Prerequisite for any new Copilot model: it must be enabled in the VS Code
// Copilot Chat model picker (Settings → Models → Manage). The Copilot proxy
// will not serve a model your account has not enabled there, regardless of
// what this file says.
//
// Upstream tier gates: Copilot subscription tier (free / pro / pro+ /
// business / enterprise / max) gates which models the proxy will serve. A
// model excluded from your tier (e.g. MAI-Code-1-Flash on enterprise as of
// 2026-06-07) cannot be unlocked locally; no models.json entry will help.
//
// To add a model, uncomment the github-copilot block below and append an
// entry with at minimum `id` and `name`. The full schema is in the installed
// pi package: <pi_install>/docs/models.md ("Model Configuration" table).
// Pi requires any provider object to specify at least one of `baseUrl`,
// `headers`, `compat`, `modelOverrides`, or a non-empty `models` array — an
// empty stub block is rejected at load time, so we leave the block out
// entirely until a model is actually being added.
//
// Verify after editing: `pi --list-models <pattern>` should resolve the new
// id. JSONC comments and trailing commas are supported.
//
// Example (do not enable until the model is exposed by Copilot to your tier
// AND enabled in your VS Code Copilot Chat picker):
//
//   "providers": {
//     "github-copilot": {
//       "models": [
//         {
//           "id": "mai-code-1-flash",
//           "name": "MAI Code 1 Flash",
//           "reasoning": false,
//           "input": ["text"],
//           "contextWindow": 200000,
//           "maxTokens": 16384
//         }
//       ]
//     }
//   }
//
// See: ADR-0026 (forward-fix pattern).
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Local inference providers (oMLX — #518, ADR-0076; local-llm ADR-009/010)
//
// The same models.json mechanism registers custom providers (pi docs/models.md
// — the documented Ollama/vLLM path). The local oMLX workhorse is installed by
// the local-llm repo's `setup-omlx-m5.sh --configure-pi`, which renders
// templates/pi-models-omlx.json into ~/.pi/agent/models.json (or leaves
// ~/.omlx/pi-provider-snippet.json for a manual merge when a providers block
// already exists).
//
// Keep this repo's copy COMMENTED: pi's /model availability check tests auth
// *presence* only (a configured `!command` apiKey counts and is never
// executed by the check), so an active block would make coding-workhorse look
// selectable on machines with no oMLX server at all. Registration is
// offline-safe on the oMLX host for the same reason — the key command runs at
// request time, so pi starts cleanly when the server is down.
//
// Known limitation of the models.json path (accepted in #518): oMLX
// context-overflow errors are not normalized to `context_length_exceeded`, so
// pi auto-compaction may not trigger on overflow. If that bites, a slim
// message_end-normalizing extension can be added without changing this
// registration.
//
//   "providers": {
//     "omlx": {
//       "baseUrl": "http://localhost:8000/v1",
//       "api": "openai-completions",
//       "apiKey": "!cat ~/.omlx/api-key",
//       "authHeader": true,
//       "compat": {
//         "supportsDeveloperRole": false,
//         "supportsReasoningEffort": false,
//         "supportsUsageInStreaming": true
//       },
//       (supportsUsageInStreaming: verified 2026-07-05 — oMLX serves full usage,
//       including cached-token detail, in the final stream chunk when pi requests
//       stream_options.include_usage; a `false` here zeroes every local turn in
//       token-meter/task-types measurement. See ADR-0077, local-llm#34.)
//       "models": [
//         {
//           "id": "coding-workhorse",
//           "name": "coding-workhorse — GLM-4.7-Flash (local oMLX)",
//           "reasoning": false,
//           "input": ["text"],
//           "contextWindow": 76800,
//           "maxTokens": 8192,
//           "cost": { "input": 0, "output": 0, "cacheRead": 0, "cacheWrite": 0 }
//         }
//       ]
//     }
//   }
//
// `id` is the oMLX ALIAS (= mlx-community/GLM-4.7-Flash-6bit, the sole pinned
// resident — local-llm ADR-010). contextWindow is deliberately held to 76800,
// far inside the 202K native window: the oMLX prefill memory guard rejects
// single prefills above ~84K tokens at its observed idle dynamic ceiling
// (66 GB; KV+SDPA ~0.433 GB/1K tokens — local-llm ADR-011, #889 ladder
// benchmark), so pi must compact before the guard 400s; concurrency is also
// prefill-activation-bound (ADR-009/010). Cost 0 ⇒ auto-router renders the
// hint "local/free"; the cost-0 routing policy is #363's scope.
{
  "providers": {}
}
